When small groups of organisms (with small populations) become separated from the main population they originated from (for example, if they migrate along a mountain range or river, or if they move to a new island, where they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in environments that differ significantly from those they previously experienced.
This new environment presents both different resources and different competitors, so new populations must possess different characteristics or adapt in order to become stronger competitors than what they previously had to contend with.
The original population has not changed at all; they still require adaptation as before.
Over time, as the new population gradually adapted to their new environment, they began to look increasingly less like the original population.
Eventually, after several thousand or even several million years, the two populations will differ so greatly that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
